WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.590
I pointed my phone at this leaf, and in

2
00:00:02.590 --> 00:00:04.810
one second it told me the plant’s name.

3
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.060
But a phone has no eyes like ours.

4
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:10.960
So what does it actually see?

5
00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:15.570
Today: how AI sees, and why it

6
00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:16.770
sometimes sees wrong.

7
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.800
Zoom right into any photo, and it breaks

8
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.820
into tiny squares called pixels.

9
00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.610
To a computer, each pixel

10
00:00:25.610 --> 00:00:26.730
is just a number.

11
00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.680
In a simple black-and-white

12
00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.080
picture, zero could mean white

13
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.320
and nine the darkest black.

14
00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:36.070
So the picture is a grid of numbers.

15
00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:39.850
Colour pictures need three numbers for

16
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:43.730
every pixel: how much red, green and blue.

17
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.720
A phone photo has about

18
00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.200
twelve million pixels.

19
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.760
That’s thirty-six million

20
00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.580
numbers in a single photo!

21
00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:55.310
So computer vision is AI that

22
00:00:55.310 --> 00:00:56.930
learns patterns in those numbers.

23
00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.870
It trains on thousands of labelled

24
00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.690
photos: leaf, ball, face.

25
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:06.160
First it learns edges, where the

26
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.120
numbers jump from light to dark.

27
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.500
Then shapes, then whole things.

28
00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.130
Think of fans in a stadium, each

29
00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.150
holding up one coloured card.

30
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.740
Up close, you just see cards.

31
00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:22.410
From across the pitch, they

32
00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:23.850
make a giant flag.

33
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.160
A single pixel means nothing either:

34
00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:29.540
the picture is in the pattern.

35
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:31.210
Quick check!

36
00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:33.870
When you take a photo, what does

37
00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:35.690
the computer actually get?

38
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.440
A grid of numbers, and nothing else.

39
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.790
No names, no meaning.

40
00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:45.250
Everything it “sees” is a pattern

41
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.390
it learned in those numbers.

42
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.200
You meet vision AI more than you think.

43
00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.370
Face unlock checks the

44
00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:53.450
patterns in your face.

45
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.440
Farm apps spot sick leaves

46
00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.200
on cassava and maize.

47
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.660
And camera translation reads a

48
00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:02.360
sign, then translates it.

49
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:04.440
Every one starts by turning

50
00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.880
the picture into numbers.

51
00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.370
But vision AI can be fooled.

52
00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.080
Dim light or harsh sun

53
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.800
changes every number.

54
00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.850
A strange angle changes the shapes.

55
00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.040
And if it never saw something in

56
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:22.040
training, like a fruit that only

57
00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.580
grows near you, it can only guess

58
00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.000
the nearest thing it knows.

59
00:02:27.380 --> 00:02:30.500
In the lab, draw on a ten-by-ten grid, or

60
00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.580
start from a smile, a house or a sun.

61
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.960
Beside it, you’ll see the same

62
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.660
picture as numbers, from zero for

63
00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.480
white to nine for darkest.

64
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.280
Make it darker, lighter, or move it one

65
00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.860
step, and watch every number change.

66
00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:48.720
Your turn.

67
00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:51.370
Face unlock works every

68
00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.490
morning in your room.

69
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.230
But outside in bright sun,

70
00:02:55.630 --> 00:02:56.450
it keeps failing.

71
00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:57.760
Why?

72
00:02:59.110 --> 00:03:00.930
The bright light changed the numbers,

73
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:02.790
so the pattern didn’t match.

74
00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.950
Remember: a picture is a grid of

75
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.790
pixels, and every pixel is a number.

76
00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.970
Vision AI learns patterns in those

77
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.530
numbers from labelled photos.

78
00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.840
And light, angles and new

79
00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:17.920
things can fool it.

80
00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:21.150
Try it yourself: draw a simple

81
00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:22.550
picture on squared paper.

82
00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.590
Write it as numbers instead, zero

83
00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.550
for white and nine for black.

84
00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.920
Give only the numbers to a friend, and see

85
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:32.520
if they can draw your picture back.
